note %global `--account` takes a profile NAME, never a path — letters, digits, `.` `_` `-`, and neither `.` nor `..`
note %global `--agent` wraps the response in {ok,data,meta,warnings,summary,breadcrumbs} — EXCEPT a `--batch` run, which stays JSONL one object per input line under `--agent`, and whose failures are those lines, so a failed run exits 3 with no {"ok":false} envelope at all; `--json` stays the RAW API body, except that `--fields` projects each row and re-serialises it through jq
note %global `--base` (and `UKU_BASE_URL`) must be `https://` unless the host is loopback — `localhost`, `127.0.0.1`, `[::1]`, `*.localhost`. An `http://` base to any other host is REFUSED before anything is sent, because the API key would go out in cleartext; so is a base with no scheme
note %global `--by-id` / `--by-name` say how to read an argument that could be either; on a WRITE an id-shaped argument that is ALSO an exact name is refused until you say which
note %global `--dump-surface` prints this table one fact per line — it is the build gate's hook, not a user feature
note %global `--fields` takes PLAIN field names (letters, digits, underscore); anything else is a usage error, because those names are handed to jq
note %global `--idempotency-key` makes a WRITE safe to repeat: the API replays the original response for 24h instead of acting twice. Each run mints its own key, so re-running a command is still two writes — pass the SAME key on the retry to make it one
note %global `--json` and `--agent` are alternatives — passing both is a usage error
note %global `--no-hints` drops the breadcrumb footer (so do `--quiet` and `--json`)
note %global `uku <command> --help --agent` emits this table as JSON, including these notes
note %global a base that is neither the default nor loopback prints one line on stderr naming the host the credentials are going to, once per invocation, and `--quiet` does not silence it
note %global these are parsed ANYWHERE on the line, before the command
note account `--account <name>` is a GLOBAL flag, NOT a subcommand here: `uku --account main tasks list`
note account `add` is sugar for `uku auth login --account <name>`
note account `remove` deletes the account's key from the OS keyring as well as the profile file, and exits 3 if the keyring would not release it
note account run `uku account current` before any write — a sandbox and the real firm look identical in the output
note api METHOD and PATH are POSITIONAL, not flags: `uku api GET /api/v3/tasks`
note api `--client NAME` works on a GET whose path is one of the collections that carry a client_id; on a write it is a usage error
note api `uku api --describe [resource]` prints the resource map — the fastest way for an agent to orient
note api this reaches every v3 endpoint, so prefer it over dropping to curl
note auth `--base` on `auth login` beats UKU_BASE_URL and the key is validated against THAT base before anything is stored
note auth `--shell` is shorthand for `--format shell`
note auth `auth logout` removes the key from BOTH the file and the OS keyring, and exits 3 if the keyring would not release it
note auth `print-header` prints a LIVE credential on stdout — never echo it back, never commit it; prefer `uku api`
note auth every keyring call is bounded by UKU_KEYRING_TIMEOUT (5s). A timeout while READING refuses the command and says the keyring is locked — it never falls back to a file. A timeout while storing during `login` DOES fall back to the 0600 file, and says so on stderr, because the alternative is losing a credential you just typed
note auth the key is kept in a 0600 file by default. UKU_KEYRING=1 puts it in the OS keyring instead (macOS `security`, Linux `secret-tool`) and leaves only the base and company in the file. It is READ from wherever the profile says it is, so unsetting the variable does not sign you out, and an existing file-stored key is never moved by an ordinary command — `uku auth login` is the only thing that migrates it, in either direction
note auth the transport rule and the non-default-host line apply to `auth login` too, and there is no extra confirmation on top of them — you named the host in the same command, and a prompt asking whether you meant what you just typed is a prompt people learn to click through
note auth what the keyring buys is exact — the key is not in a file this CLI writes, so whatever can read your files does not get it. It does NOT stop you, or anything running as you, from asking the keyring for it
note batch `uku batch` is not how you run a batch — that is `uku <res> create --batch @f.jsonl`
note batch reads the LOCAL ledger under $UKU_CONFIG_HOME/batches and sends no request
note clients --batch belongs to `create`; `uku clients list --batch` is a usage error, not a bulk read
note clients `--client` is not a filter here (you are already listing clients) — use `--q`
note clients `get`/`patch` also take a NAME or a pasted URL — see the `--client` note on `tasks`
note clients a write needs --yes when stdout is not a TTY (exit 4 otherwise)
note clients on `patch`, an id-shaped argument costs one extra GET: it is checked against client NAMES too, because `12345678` and `CAFE-1` are both — `--by-id` skips the check
note completions eval "$(uku completions zsh)" in your rc
note contracts a contract has no name field, so `get` takes an id or a pasted URL — never a name
note contracts read-only in the CLI; contract rows are ETag-guarded — see `uku help api` and the 428 rules
note doctor `--json` (the global flag) gives the whole report as JSON
note doctor exits 1 only when a REQUIRED check fails, never for a warning
note doctor it names which credential store the active account uses — the OS keyring, the 0600 file, or the environment — which is the one thing about UKU_KEYRING worth never guessing at
note health asks the API if it is up — NEEDS NO CREDENTIAL, so it answers before `uku auth login` has ever run
note health exits 0 up, 3 the API answered but is unwell, 5 could not be reached at all
note health this is the "is it me or is it them" check; `uku doctor` is the "is it my setup" one
note help `uku <command> --help` prints the same card
note help a topic with no card is a usage error, not an empty page
note invoices an invoice has no name field, so `get` takes an id or a pasted URL — never a name
note invoices money needs an All-scope key — a `403 … scope` is the system working as designed, do not retry around it
note invoices there is no `uku invoices patch`; use `uku api PATCH /api/v3/invoices/<id>`
note invoices there is no `uku invoices send` and no `uku invoices mark-paid` either — the API has both as POSTs, so use `uku api POST /api/v3/invoices/<id>/send --yes` and `uku api POST /api/v3/invoices/<id>/mark-paid --yes`. Do not guess a subcommand for a money action
note me prints exactly what `uku auth status` prints — the identity behind the active key
note members `get` also takes a member NAME (resolved through `--q`) or a pasted URL
note members read-only in the CLI — member writes go through `uku api`
note products `get` also takes a product NAME or a pasted URL
note products read-only in the CLI; the API does accept `POST /api/v3/products` (needs `name`) — reach it with `uku api`
note projects `get` also takes a project NAME or a pasted URL
note projects read-only in the CLI; the API does accept `POST /api/v3/projects` (needs `name`) — reach it with `uku api`
note search NOT a server-side global search — the API offers none this CLI can verify. It is ONE `--q` fanned out over the list endpoints that have a name field (clients, tasks, members, products, projects), one request each
note search `--limit` is per endpoint
note search a resource that errors is reported as FAILED, never folded into "0 results" — exit 3 when any did
note setup UKU_SETUP_AGENT=claude|cursor|codex|all|none picks the target; the default `auto` detects what is installed
note tasks NAME resolution never happens in a `--batch`, and never for an argument that already looks like an id — but on a WRITE an id-shaped argument is still PROBED for a record whose name is that exact string (one extra GET), and a collision is refused until `--by-id` or `--by-name` says which was meant; on a read the id-shaped argument passes straight through with no request
note tasks `--client NAME|ID` resolves the name once and sends `client_id=<id>` — the same query pair you would type by hand
note tasks `create` needs `title`; status/date/client_id are optional
note tasks a 428 on `POST /api/v3/tasks` is NOT healed — it is a collection POST with no id to read an ETag from
note tasks a name lookup reads at most 100 records and compares `meta.total` against them: when more could exist than were examined it refuses, because a match it cannot see the alternatives to is not a unique one
note tasks an argument that is not id-shaped is resolved by NAME through the same `--q` a list takes: one match is used and named on stderr, several REFUSE and list the candidates
note tasks that the endpoint HONOURS client_id is the one thing here that could not be verified offline, so it is MEASURED: any returned row whose client_id differs from the one asked for means the filter was not applied, and the command fails with exit 3 instead of handing back an unfiltered list. A row with no client_id (or a null one) proves nothing and never trips it, and `--client` refuses up front without jq because that comparison is what it needs
note time `create` needs task_id, person_id and start
note time `start` with no `end` is a RUNNING TIMER, not logged work — the CLI refuses duration-without-end before sending
note time a time entry has no name, so `get` takes an id or a pasted URL — never a name
note time there is NO `uku time patch` — use `uku api PATCH /api/v3/time-entries/<id>`
note update re-runs the installer for the newest RELEASE tag, checksum-verified; UKU_CHANNEL=main opts into the unverified rolling edge
note version identical to `uku --version`
